{"format_version":"vbulletin-3+-thread","url":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/werewolf-the-forsaken/533623-a-werewolf-and-a-spirit-get-into-a-fight","id":533623,"title":"A werewolf and a spirit get into a fight...","path":["Home","Onyx Path","White Wolf Worlds","Chronicles of Darkness","Werewolf: The Forsaken"],"posts":[{"date":"2015-05-30T14:18:50-07:00","id":533623,"title":"A werewolf and a spirit get into a fight...","content":"<br /> My ironmaster stumbles onto a rank 2 spirit feeding at a hidden loci. The irked spirit knows that a werewolf arriving often means it's about to get chased away. The spirit sees only one werewolf, standing in the dark and unaware of its presence. It attacks. <br /> <br /> As far as skills and abilities go, I based the spirit on the &quot;shadow wraith&quot; rank 2 spirit from the book (page 202ish). <br /> <br /> The werewolf started in hishu (human). For two rounds, the spirit whomped on him hard and I was almost pulling punches because I was worried I overdid it. But after two rounds the werewolf dropped into kuruth (death rage in gauru form) because of damage from the spirit. Once it hit kuruth it healed the damage and proceed to wipe the floor with the spirit. Once the spirit fled the werewolf tore the place apart and eventually came to. <br /> <br /> This was an interesting conflict to me because it was one wolf, basically on autopilot, and he needed to do nothing to win this fight except wait to get damaged enough to death rage.<br /> <br /> Is this pretty much everyone else's experience? I get the feeling a rank 3 spirit would have likely killed the wolf, but a rank 2 had little chance unless it had strong situation modifiers or a really lucky string of rolls. <br /> <br /> This topic is important to me so that I can plan encounters that aren't toothless for the players, but at the same time aren't impossibly lethal.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/5220-mother-s-love","name":"Mother's Love","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar5220_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Mar 2015"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"58"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2015-05-30T14:33:35-07:00","id":533649,"title":"","content":"This is why most spirits gotta respect Uratha. Also why most would, in that case, simply hide within the area of influence of the locus rather than attack an Uratha on it's own.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/576-ephsy","name":"Ephsy","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar576_2.png","title":"Banned","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"2615"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2015-05-30T14:41:47-07:00","id":533653,"title":"","content":"Right! I suppose you might say the rank 2 spirit this time around was 'feeling lucky'. But from a mechanics and planning point of view, during the first couple of rounds the spirit was doing so much damage that I was a little alarmed as the ST. Once the wolf death raged it became clear that the wolf was going to win this fight from the start.<br /> <br /> Still, if this had been a rank 3 spirit, I think it may have gone very differently for the werewolf. <br /> <br /> I'm curious if others have this impression for planning a spirit encounter against a lone werewolf, or have had other experiences with spirit vs werewolf encounters they want to share.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/5220-mother-s-love","name":"Mother's Love","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar5220_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Mar 2015"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"58"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2015-05-30T14:52:04-07:00","id":533659,"title":"","content":"Usually? I'm less concerned about what may cause the Kuruth than what the consequences are for it. Sure, you likely discorporate the spirit (which may or may not come back to haunt you) but, did your rampage end there? if not, which spirit did you offend with it? Which helpless human being became your prey merely on account of being in the worst place at the worst time?","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/576-ephsy","name":"Ephsy","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar576_2.png","title":"Banned","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"2615"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2015-05-30T17:27:15-07:00","id":533780,"title":"","content":"I understand and appreciate that the underlying story is king when it comes to planning and playing out a scene - particularly when you 'lose control of the beast' and must endure the consequence. Still, mechanics are there to give guidance to game play, and particularly in how conflict is resolved. What I notice is that in my game a single wolf will likely wind up deathraging when fighting a spirit (without knowledge of their ban, the opportunity to plan or some other significant situation modifier). <br /> <br /> So have you noticed this or had a different experience?<br />","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/5220-mother-s-love","name":"Mother's Love","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar5220_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Mar 2015"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"58"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2015-05-31T02:15:26-07:00","id":534147,"title":"","content":"Does sufficient non-aggravated damage still trigger Kuruth in 2e? I don't recall that being one of the triggers.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/3166-elfive","name":"Elfive","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar3166_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Jun 2014"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"8226"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2015-05-31T03:51:58-07:00","id":534203,"title":"","content":"Taking damage from anything other than silver does not normally cause Death Rage, unless you have chosen a specific Trigger set that does cause it.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/>- Chris Allen, Aberrant Line Developer, Freelance Writer<br /> <br /> \u200bLike my work? Feel like helping me stay supplied with tea? Check out my <b><a href=\"https://www.patreon.com/acrozatarim\" target=\"_blank\">Patreon</a></b>","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/1757-acrozatarim","name":"Acrozatarim","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar1757_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Dec 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"1892"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2015-05-31T05:08:31-07:00","id":534213,"title":"","content":"Also no reason to fall to Kuruth to take out the spirit, the rage of garou form functions just fine without being death frenzied.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/3347-wild-eyes","name":"Wild-Eyes","avatar":"data:image/svg+xml;base64,","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Jun 2014"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"121"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2015-05-31T05:47:53-07:00","id":534233,"title":"","content":"I think ideally you'd want a werewolf to be roughly equal to a spirit of the same rank. Not sure how often that works in practice though.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/3166-elfive","name":"Elfive","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar3166_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Jun 2014"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"8226"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2015-05-31T06:36:09-07:00","id":534274,"title":"","content":"By virtue of their special natures, I've usually found werewolves are roughly equal to spirits one Rank higher than themselves at Ranks 1-3, and equal (or slightly inferior) to spirits of equivalent Rank at 4-5. (Rank 3 can go either way depending on the spirit's focus and how advantageous the terrain.)","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/><i>Writer. Developer.</i> <b>World of Darkness</b> | <b>Chronicles of Darkness</b> | <b>The Trinity Continuum</b>","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/156-bunyip","name":"Bunyip","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar156_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"1880"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2015-05-31T11:35:17-07:00","id":534647,"title":"","content":"Preparing for combat in werewolf, really all World of Darkness games, can be difficult, especially with death rage as a fallback. If the attacker has a decent-sized dice pool they could potentially fill a character's lethal in one shot. Hedging your bets with less dice, and you run a risk of plinking the character with one damage a pop. Then with an entire group, or multiple attackers, and the chance of death rises. Which, really, is probably pretty accurate. But it does make it hard to balance a threat from a distraction. Werewolf and vampire both have some good ways to protect players from accidental death while not making them immune to all harm, with vampire's damage reduction and werewolf's death rage and ability to ensure the 'lesser enemies' don't pose a threat to gauru form. Hopefully the upcoming Hurt Locker will have some good advice plotting things out.<br /> <br /> But I think the idea for werewolf is that rank 1 and 2 spirits, the hursihim, aren't meant to be werewolf killers on their own, outside of unique circumstances, such as one coming across a weakened werewolf or being a murder or silver spirit. They are the children and animals of the spirit kingdom, and while both can be deadly, in general they are too base to do so. Though it's probably important to note that if there were enough of them, en masse they might be able to slaughter an entire, low ranked pack.<br /> <br /> Rank 3 is where they start getting their independence and developing more complex thought, and cunning. I could easily see one killing a single werewolf providing it really wanted to, through use of Numina and influences, even recruiting hursihim to act as ephemera shields.<br /> <br /> I want to agree with Bunyip, but I think part of the problem is going to be the difference between a pack of werewolves and a lone wolf, as well as a group of spirits and an individual, which can really skew the whole thing in a drastically different way. But I wouldn't be surprised at all if a lone werewolf was able to beat one or two rank 1 or 2 spirits or even a rank three through the use of death rage. Werewolves are dangerous, even to spirits, and death rage is a werewolf at its most dangerous. Plus it would suck if your characters, as scions of Father Wolf, were getting killed by the spirit equivalent of animals and children.<br /> <br /> As for dealing with Kuruth as an 'instant win' button in the hands of the werewolves, against certain foes, especially weaker ones, that's the way it's going to work. But the benefit of the black out for death rage is that the smarter foes, like rank 3 and up spirits, can run or hide, allowing players to wake up without knowing whether or not they've defeated their foes. With the ability to hide in the zone of influence of a locus or simply run away, characters can jump to conclusions as to whether they're victorious or not. And if you wanted to be particularly nasty, the spirit could just wait until a pack is all passed out and in hishu form until it reveals itself and resumes its ass-kicking.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":886,"user_link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/886-nofather","user":"nofather","date":". 05-31-2015, 02:55 PM"},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/886-nofather","name":"nofather","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar886_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"11016"}]},"thankedby":[]}]}